Date: 2026-09-25. Reviewer: agent:g3-reviewer. These checks refine, but do not
substitute for, bundle-bound verification. The bundle remains the review input.

1. OpenStax Psychology 2e, Section 8.1 "How memory functions"
   https://openstax.org/books/psychology-2e/pages/8-1-how-memory-functions
   Method: WebFetch (live page), 2026-09-25.
   Result: The page does NOT contain study-strategy evidence. No spacing/spaced/
   distributed practice, no retrieval practice/testing effect, no re-reading,
   no highlighting, no teaching-it-to-someone study content. Closest content:
   "Active rehearsal is a way of attending to information to move it from
   short-term to long-term memory"; elaborative rehearsal ("the act of linking
   new information you are trying to learn to existing information that you
   already know"); levels of processing (Craik & Lockhart, 1972). Decay and
   proactive interference ARE present ("During decay, the memory trace becomes
   less activated over time..."; "During proactive interference, previously
   learned information interferes with the ability to learn new information");
   retrieval failure is not presented as a cause of forgetting; no Ebbinghaus.
   Consequence for findings: the unit's comparative study-strategy evidence
   claims (topic-02.mdx spacing/retrieval passages; fig-U4-4 "what the evidence
   says" column) are absent from the cited source itself, not merely from the
   bound excerpt.

